WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.471
Lesson 2 clears up a very common confusion.

2
00:00:06.471 --> 00:00:12.942
Students often associate energy with motion alone, but gravitational potential energy belongs to position in a field.

3
00:00:12.942 --> 00:00:19.413
In the M3 language, Height Store can rise even when the pod is standing still, as long as it has been lifted.

4
00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:25.884
That is why the lesson keeps three factors on the board at once: load, deck level, and World Grip.

5
00:00:25.884 --> 00:00:32.355
Height alone is not enough.

6
00:00:32.355 --> 00:00:38.825
The store belongs to the whole mgh story, and the worked example becomes much easier when the student keeps all three factors visible instead of collapsing the idea to just going higher.

7
00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:45.296
Keep this takeaway in view: Height Store belongs to position in a field, and it grows with all three factors in the mgh relationship.

8
00:00:45.296 --> 00:00:51.767
Still but energized means position matters.

9
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:58.238
A raised object can hold more Height Store even without motion.

10
00:00:58.238 --> 00:01:04.709
Three-factor rule means m g h.

11
00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:11.180
Mass, field strength, and height change all matter together.

12
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:17.769
This diagram matters because it slows the learner down in exactly the right way.

13
00:01:17.769 --> 00:01:24.358
If mass changes while height and World Grip stay the same, the store changes.

14
00:01:24.358 --> 00:01:30.947
If height changes while mass and World Grip stay the same, the store changes.

15
00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:37.536
If World Grip changes while the other two stay fixed, the store changes again.

16
00:01:37.536 --> 00:01:44.124
The board turns mgh into a living comparison rather than a symbol string.

17
00:01:44.124 --> 00:01:50.713
That is important because many worked examples are really variable-control questions in disguise.

18
00:01:50.713 --> 00:01:57.302
Keep this takeaway in view: The diagram turns Height Store into a controlled-comparison lesson instead of a single memorized formula.

19
00:01:57.302 --> 00:02:03.891
Load: A more massive pod stores more energy at the same height in the same field.

20
00:02:03.891 --> 00:02:10.480
Deck level: A bigger height change means a bigger gain in Height Store.

21
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:16.754
Imagine packages resting on different shelves in the same warehouse.

22
00:02:16.754 --> 00:02:23.028
None of them is moving, but the one on the higher shelf has a different lifting story behind it.

23
00:02:23.028 --> 00:02:29.302
Add a heavier package or a stronger lifting environment, and the energy accounting changes again.

24
00:02:29.302 --> 00:02:35.576
This analogy matters because it helps students stop treating energy as motion-only.

25
00:02:35.576 --> 00:02:41.850
Height Store is about where the object is in the gravitational setting, not about whether it is currently moving.

26
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:48.124
Keep this takeaway in view: The shelf analogy helps students see that stillness does not mean zero energy when position in a field has changed.

27
00:02:48.124 --> 00:02:54.398
Higher shelf means more lifting history.

28
00:02:54.398 --> 00:03:00.672
Greater height means more gravitational potential energy when the other factors stay fixed.

29
00:03:00.672 --> 00:03:06.946
Heavier package means bigger store.

30
00:03:06.946 --> 00:03:13.220
More mass means more energy stored for the same shelf level and field.

31
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:18.743
The formal statement is gravitational potential energy equals mgh.

32
00:03:18.743 --> 00:03:24.266
That means Height Store depends on mass, gravitational field strength, and height.

33
00:03:24.266 --> 00:03:29.789
The lesson becomes much stronger when the learner reads that formula qualitatively before calculating.

34
00:03:29.789 --> 00:03:35.312
Double the mass and the store doubles if the other factors stay fixed.

35
00:03:35.312 --> 00:03:40.835
Double the height and the store doubles.

36
00:03:40.835 --> 00:03:46.358
Double the field strength and the store doubles.

37
00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:51.882
Those comparison moves are what make the equation meaningful.

38
00:03:51.882 --> 00:03:57.405
Keep this takeaway in view: The mgh formula is strongest when it is read as a three-factor model before it is used as a calculator input.

39
00:03:57.405 --> 00:04:02.928
Qualitative use: The formula helps compare what happens when one factor changes and the others do not.

40
00:04:02.928 --> 00:04:08.451
Quantitative use: It also gives a direct route to the energy value when m, g, and h are known.

41
00:04:08.451 --> 00:04:13.974
gravitational potential energy = mgh.

42
00:04:13.974 --> 00:04:19.497
mass, field strength, and height all matter together.

43
00:04:19.497 --> 00:04:25.020
Use the formula after deciding which of the three factors is changing and which are fixed.

44
00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.825
A useful pattern here is to freeze two factors and change one.

45
00:04:29.825 --> 00:04:34.631
Hold height and field strength fixed while changing mass.

46
00:04:34.631 --> 00:04:39.436
Then reset and hold mass and field strength fixed while changing height.

47
00:04:39.436 --> 00:04:44.242
Then reset again and vary World Grip.

48
00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:49.047
This routine matters because it makes causal reasoning visible.

49
00:04:49.047 --> 00:04:53.853
Instead of just saying mgh, the learner sees what each part of mgh does.

50
00:04:53.853 --> 00:04:58.658
Keep this takeaway in view: When students freeze two factors and change one, the Height Store equation stops feeling like a blur.

51
00:04:58.658 --> 00:05:03.464
Pass 1 means change mass only.

52
00:05:03.464 --> 00:05:08.269
This isolates the effect of load on the Height Store.

53
00:05:08.269 --> 00:05:13.075
Pass 2 means change height only.

54
00:05:13.075 --> 00:05:17.880
This isolates the effect of deck level or height change.

55
00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.289
Try four checks.

56
00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:24.699
Can a stationary raised pod still have more Height Store?

57
00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:28.108
Yes.

58
00:05:28.108 --> 00:05:31.518
Does the store depend only on height?

59
00:05:31.518 --> 00:05:34.927
No.

60
00:05:34.927 --> 00:05:38.336
If height doubles while mass and World Grip stay the same, what happens?

61
00:05:38.336 --> 00:05:41.746
The store doubles.

62
00:05:41.746 --> 00:05:45.155
And if World Grip changes, should it stay in the story?

63
00:05:45.155 --> 00:05:48.564
Yes, absolutely.

64
00:05:48.564 --> 00:05:51.974
Those are the key habits behind the lesson.

65
00:05:51.974 --> 00:05:55.383
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the Height Store is now a clear three-factor story instead of a half-remembered equation.

66
00:05:55.383 --> 00:05:58.792
Still pod means yes, can store energy.

67
00:05:58.792 --> 00:06:02.202
Position in the field matters even without motion.

68
00:06:02.202 --> 00:06:05.611
Height only?

69
00:06:05.611 --> 00:06:09.021
means no.

70
00:06:09.021 --> 00:06:12.430
Mass and field strength belong in the explanation as well.

71
00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:17.872
You are ready for the worked example now.

72
00:06:17.872 --> 00:06:23.313
Start by naming the three factors in the Height Store story.

73
00:06:23.313 --> 00:06:28.755
Decide which one is changing and which are fixed.

74
00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:34.197
Then write the mgh statement and substitute carefully.

75
00:06:34.197 --> 00:06:39.638
If the question is comparative rather than numerical, keep the factor logic visible in words before you calculate.

76
00:06:39.638 --> 00:06:45.080
That is the habit this lesson is trying to build.

77
00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.522
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: Height Store can now be reasoned about qualitatively and quantitatively with the same framework.

78
00:06:50.522 --> 00:06:55.963
Step 1: Read the physical situation as a position-in-a-field story.

79
00:06:55.963 --> 00:07:01.405
Step 2: Keep all three factors on the page, even if only one is changing.

80
00:07:01.405 --> 00:07:06.847
identify mass, field strength, and height in the story.

81
00:07:06.847 --> 00:07:12.288
decide which factors are fixed and which one is changing.

82
00:07:12.288 --> 00:07:17.730
The best answers keep the three-factor reasoning visible before the arithmetic begins.
